european leaders are stepping up efforts  to prevent russia from invading ukraine  french president emmanuel macro is  leading a new diplomatic effort to  engage all parties in dialogue he's been  holding talks here in berlin after  meetings in kiev and moscow macau says  there's been no breakthrough but that  he's helped defuse tensions  moscow denies claiming he's planning an  attack but is continuing to amass  weapons and troops at the border with  ukraine  in the palaces of power europe's leaders  are engaging in a diplomatic push to  defuse the crisis on ukraine's borders  the leaders of germany france and poland  tried to show a united front  in berlin  our common goal is to prevent a war in  europe  the deployment of russian troops on the  border with ukraine is very worrying and  our assessment here is very much the  same as is our position  the meeting followed a diplomatic blitz  by emmanuel macron who pushed for  further talks on visits to moscow and  kiev  i believe that we have concrete  practical solutions that will allow us  to move forward  we have had the opportunity to discuss  this together  i have also had the opportunity to  discuss this with president putin as i  have done with my european partners and  several of our allies  one of those practical solutions could  be resolving a long-running conflict  within ukraine's own borders  in the eastern donbass region ukrainian  soldiers have been fighting  russian-backed separatists  over seven years ago france and germany  helped negotiate a peace deal between  ukraine the separatist forces and their  backer russia  but the ceasefire agreements the  so-called minsk accords never held  now experts think these diplomatic  channels and the minsk agreements could  be revived  but a reduction in weapons and troops  along ukraine's border and in eastern  europe looks unlikely  the kremlin denied reports that putin  told macron russia would not further  increase military tensions and pull  troops out of belarus later this month  meanwhile more planes carrying u.s  soldiers touched down in nato member  poland  while the diplomatic channels are still  open the build-up of soldiers in the  region continues  well let's talk about some of the  developments with theresa fallon  director of the center for russian  europe asia studies in brussels uh we've  heard from macau  saying that they have concrete practical  solutions that will allow them to move  forward do you have any idea what he's  talking about there  well that's quite interesting because it  seems that the kremlin has already tried  to step back what macron has said that  he got out of the kremlin so i think  that's a worrying sign uh also after he  went to kiev they both  president zielinski of ukraine and also  president macron stepped away from the  idea of the finlandization that's almost  a pejorative about trying to put ukraine  on this path maybe they did discuss this  but i think in the public domain that is  something extremely sensitive and  because of eight years of war  and 14 000 deaths in ukraine it's  actually made ukraine more pro-western  so this idea might have reached its  sell-by date uh this idea of a  finlandization of ukraine is so  sensitive because  there's also this kind of growing  nationalism in ukraine because of eight  years of war maybe these stuff should  have been taken far earlier and in  addition to that i mean putin couldn't  resist taking a little jibe at uh  president macron saying that after five  year five hours of dialogue with him it  was torture so it's kind of a mixed  message that the kremlin is sending in  regard to macron's meeting with him  what about this talk of uh proposing a  neutral status for ukraine in order to  satisfy russia's security demands what  do you make of that  as i i just mentioned that i think that  uh this is one possible off-ramp but  will the ukrainian public agree to that  and this also sends worrying messages to  both sweden and finland because they  fear that the door to nato could be  closing their status is neutral and what  will be the bigger security implications  for the region and many analysts say  that  if you give that  now to putin he will ask for more in the  region because let's remember his two uh  grand  agreements that he's asked nato and the  us  for is that all troops move out of  central and eastern europe  since 1997 any country that joined nato  so this might just be the hors d'oeuvre  to a much bigger change in the security  landscape  russia and belarus are conducting joint  drills in belarus how do you assess  this in in light of the crisis  well i think it sends an extremely  worrying message because if you look at  the map kiev is not very far at all from  belarus and in addition to that  president macron said that was one of  the things that he received from  president putin that there would be some  form of de-escalation because that's  what everyone wants to see in order to  continue dialogue but president putin  didn't really say that that would happen  he says that he's moving troops they're  doing exercises nothing to worry about  here but if you look at the strategic  map there i mean that's an incredible  pincer movement and it means that uh  perhaps belarus is far more in  moscow's orbit than other people are  comfortable with especially other  countries in the region what about the  western buildup of troops then which has  begun is that the right signal to be  sending  well right now there are about 130 000  troops that russia has put into place  the west is kind of putting in 300  troops to germany for example so i think  it's it's a sign of assurance because  these are members of nato but i think  that this doesn't even come close to  what russia is doing  teresa fallon director of the center for  russia  europe asia studies thank you very much  for being on the show appreciate it  thank you  thomas silverhorn is a member of the  german bundestag for the conservative  csu and is the parliamentary group  spokesperson for transatlantic ties  welcome  macau says we have concrete practical  solutions that will allow us to move  forward what are those solutions  our first it was high time for the  german chancellor to be in washington  and to dispel any doubts on the new  german government but clear messages we  stand united in the transatlantic  community there is a close coordination  also in details  and yes everything is at the table and  what we need now is a clear and concise  messaging towards russia in order to  bring them to the table of negotiations  what is going to bring them to the table  though what's going to get that dialogue  continuing and um  and some sort of solution found  well everything on the table means we  have to coordinate ourselves within the  transatlantic community  on very concrete steps  including economic sanctions including  the entire energy supply  including also the delivery of weapons  not every partner has to do everything  but we have to have a clear concept and  a clear messaging and yes  talking and walking have to go hand in  hand now it's been rumored that mccoy is  proposing a neutral status for ukraine  to satisfy russia's security demands  what do you make of that  we have to be aware of the fact that  putin follows a long-term strategic  vision of restoring a sort of former  former soviet union  and at this point we have to state that  there is a systemic difference between  liberal democracies and autocracy  between the western integrated countries  in the european union and nato and  russia  and here we can only state what has  already been stated in the 70s of the  last century we can only agree to  disagree at this systemic difference  but bearing in mind  those systemic  differences we have to find uh motors  with nd how to proceed and now it's up  to  the negotiating parties  to sit together and to define a concept  and an object for negotiations how to  shape  the entire security architecture but  it's not up to  any one of  the western leaders i would say  to proceed with the single proposals we  should stay united and bear in mind the  long vision the long-term vision of  russia  mr zuberhorn what would a neutral status  for ukraine actually mean though what  would that do  it's up to the ukraine to decide  for themselves where they want to go  with european  integration with democracy with the rule  of law  and russia now  sets  the entire international order at stake  if the russian concept is to dominate  its neighboring countries  to  to attack  the sovereignty and territorial  integrity of neighboring countries  this is not a common ground for  negotiations so we have to strive to  stick to the international  public order and this is the core  essence of the messaging we should send  as  partners in the transatlantic community  there's certainly a lot at stake here  and so much more thomas silverhorn from  the german bundestag thank you very much  for being on the show  thank you  for more let's bring in bruno tata here  he's the deputy director of the  foundation for strategic research that's  a french independent thank tank thanks  for being with us brina multiple  diplomatic initiatives are underway to  resolve the russia ukraine crisis would  you say that france under emmanuel  macron is now the main player in leading  those efforts for europe  well not the main player but clearly  macron thought he needed to take the  initiative  i think he does not anticipate that he  will be the only leader  talking to putin and zielinski but  clearly his initiative which is to be  followed by a visit by chancellor  schultz later  in a few days  has triggered some kind of diplomatic  momentum so i would say he's been  leading the way but he's certainly not  the only  european leader who's going to play an  important role in trying to solve this  crisis  so you say momentum is now building in  these diplomatic efforts uh president  mcconn claims he received assurances  from president putin that a  de-escalation in the russia ukraine  crisis is possible now russia has been  downplaying any talk of progress what's  your reading of the outcome of mr  mcconnell's shuttle diplomacy this week  well two things first of all  there's a value in diplomacy in itself  meeting face-to-face a leader as mccoy's  done and as schultz will do  for four five six hours it has value in  itself because it allows the leaders to  better gauge each other and know each  other's attention i'm not saying that  that  as former president bush once said but  there is a value in itself in the meet  in the long meeting face-to-face second  there's a war there's a war of  communication that's a classic you would  anticipate  russia saying something like of course  we made no commitments whatsoever well i  say let's wait and see uh ishmael is  correct to say that  russia has promised to withdraw his  forces from belarus after the exercises  that are going to take place in the  coming days  then it would be very simple to uh to  verify  okay uh president macron made reference  though to concrete practical solutions  for the ukraine crisis and for  addressing russia's security concerns  what exactly  do you think he's talking about there  he's talking about two different things  you see the two separate tracks that  both france germany poland and the  united states and others are trying to  explore there's one tribe which is the  circle normandy format which is about  the implementation of the minsk two  agreements it's about the domestic  future of ukraine and according to his  credit has been uh  successful in rejuvenating a format  which everybody thought back in november  that was that it was pretty much dead so  that's the first strike the other track  is the more the broader discussion on  the so-called security architecture in  europe and this is about  arms control this is about confidence  and security building measures and  that's the discussion that's obviously  also taking place at the u.s russian  level and the nato russia  level but there's a european input in  that discussion and that's what i was  referring to is that enough to defuse  the crisis uh what do i know  it all depends of uh what  mr putin's plans are and nobody knows  what they are and i'm even sure that  mr macron does not know much more  as he returns to paris  just briefly do you see a  coherent strategy for shaping the  european security architecture between  europe the u.s and nato in a way that  would satisfy russia  well the first part of the answer to  your question is yes there is a coherent  strategies that despite what many  commentators say  uh europe and the united states france  germany and the us are pretty much on  the same page here there are nuances of  course but i don't see any significant  divergence of opinion or political  choices regarding these big issues and  that's what really matters there's  there's a unity of europe and there's a  unity of the west behind the nuances now  do we think that we can produce  options and solutions that are  acceptable both to us and to mr putin  unfortunately i don't think so and this  is where deterrence comes into play  because uh even if mr putin does not yet  hasn't yet decided to uh to embark in a  major military operation in ukraine we  have to deter him to discourage him from  thinking he could achieve meaningful  military results without suffering  extraordinary severe policies  deputy director of the foundation for  strategic research thank you very much  for talking with us  thank you  you

